# ─── Neo4j Aura ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# From the .txt credentials file Aura makes you download when you create the
# instance. It is the ONLY time Aura shows you the password, so keep that file.
#
# Copy all FOUR values out of that file rather than assuming any of them.
# NEO4J_USERNAME and NEO4J_DATABASE are usually "neo4j" — but not always. Some
# instances use the instance id (the 8 characters at the start of the hostname)
# for both, and when they do, the only symptom is:
#
#     Neo.ClientError.Security.Unauthorized — the client is unauthorized
#
# which reads exactly like a wrong password and sends you looking in the wrong
# place. `make verify` fails fast on this and prints the other candidate.
NEO4J_URI=neo4j+s://xxxxxxxx.databases.neo4j.io
NEO4J_USERNAME=neo4j
NEO4J_PASSWORD=
NEO4J_DATABASE=neo4j

# ─── OpenAI ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# The chat model used by the text2cypher chain and the agent.
# `make verify` proves this one works by calling it, and prints the alternatives
# if the API rejects it. Model IDs move fast; this is a knob, not a constant.
#
# Careful: the model list and the models you can actually call are not the same
# set. Bare `gpt-5.6` works and resolves to gpt-5.6-sol, but does not appear in
# models.list(). Name the variant you want explicitly and there is no ambiguity.
#
# Pick this one deliberately. Measured on this workshop's own data against the
# grounded-answer prompt of the day — then LangChain's GraphCypherQAChain QA
# prompt, now steps/_common.py ANSWER_PROMPT. The numbers below were taken
# against the former, so treat the terra/luna rows as indicative rather than
# current; sol was re-checked against the new prompt and still refuses nothing.
#
# That the prompt matters this much is the point, not a footnote. neo4j-graphrag
# ships a deliberately bare default ("Context: ... Question: ... Answer:") with
# no instruction that the context is authoritative, and under it even sol
# declined the indirect-dependency question with four correct rows in hand.
# ANSWER_PROMPT exists to say the thing LangChain's prompt used to say for you.
#
# Refusal-on-correct-data rates:
#
#                     short context   long context (real step 04 rows)
#     gpt-5.6-sol         0/8              0/6     <- pinned below
#     gpt-5.6-terra       0/8              6/6
#     gpt-5.6-luna        8/8              8/8
#
# The failure mode is the nasty kind: the Cypher is correct and the rows come
# back, and the model declines to answer from them, replying "I don't know".
# Luna does it on everything; terra does it once the retrieved rows get long,
# which is exactly what happens on the "What is Alice working on?" question.
# Sol has not done it on anything tried. See FACILITATOR.md.
#
# Note the awkward part, if you are tempted to optimise for price: the cheap
# models are the broken ones. Per 1M tokens, in/out:
#
#     gpt-5.6-sol      $5.00 / $30.00
#     gpt-5.5          $5.00 / $30.00   same price, also reliable here
#     gpt-5.6-terra    $2.00 / $12.00   (cut 20% in July 2026)  refuses 6/6
#     gpt-5.6-luna     $0.20 /  $1.20   (cut 80% in July 2026)  refuses 8/8
#
# Luna is 25x cheaper and cannot do this job. gpt-5.5 is a fine substitute for
# sol at identical cost if you prefer the older, more widely documented model.
#
# The whole workshop is roughly 30 chat calls per attendee at ~400 input and
# ~110 output tokens each, so about $0.15 a head. Cost is not the constraint;
# picking a model that answers is.
OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5.6-sol

# Reasoning effort.
#
# Measured on this graph, this knob does nothing — which is itself worth knowing
# before you spend the workshop tuning it. It barely moves cost either: reasoning
# tokens on the synthesis prompt averaged 6 / 35 / 59 at low / medium / high,
# against a 339-token input. Going from low to high costs about 30 extra output
# tokens per call, or a tenth of a cent across the whole workshop.
#
# With gpt-5.6-sol, sweeping low/medium/high changed neither correctness nor
# latency:
#
#   synthesis (answering from returned rows)   0 refusals at all three levels
#                                              3.9s / 3.1s / 3.0s per call
#   Cypher generation (4 hard questions x3)    same queries, same answers
#                                              4.5s / 4.6s / 4.8s per call
#
# So `low` is kept as the cheap default rather than as a latency workaround, and
# a split (low to write Cypher, higher to synthesise) is not worth the extra
# moving part here. On a graph with a real schema the split may well pay — this
# one has 4 labels and 5 relationship types, which is not enough to strain it.
#
# What DOES change the outcome is which model you pick. See above.
OPENAI_REASONING_EFFORT=low

# ─── Embeddings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DO NOT CHANGE THIS unless you also change it in the Aura Agent vector tool.
# Aura Agent's similarity-search tool only accepts:
#   text-embedding-3-small | text-embedding-3-large | text-embedding-ada-002
# (or the Vertex AI equivalents). Picking anything else — a local model, Cohere —
# silently breaks the no-code half of this workshop, because the Aura Agent
# cannot re-embed a query into the same vector space as your index.
EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small

# Name of the vector index. The Aura Agent vector tool refers to it by this name,
# so it has to match on both sides.
VECTOR_INDEX_NAME=task_embeddings
